From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: allwinner: A733 display engine -- DRAM into TCON_TV1

The block that reads pixels out of memory. `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source`
gave this SoC a raster and `drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream` gave it a
DisplayPort stream, and the picture on the monitor so far comes out of
TCON_TV1's own VDBIST pattern generator with no framebuffer in the path.
TV_SRC_CTL_REG bit 0 has exactly two settings -- the DE provides the data
source (0), or the VDBIST test-pattern generator does (1) -- so this patch
writes the other source. The datapath is driven by the KMS master only.

What it does, and what it leaves at reset
-----------------------------------------

One display pipe, one VI channel, one layer, XRGB8888, the 1920x1200 mode in
sun60i-display.h that the TCON and the transmitter already share. The driver
allocates its own DMA-coherent framebuffer and paints it, so the fetch path is
provable with no KMS device above it -- the same shape the TCON patch used.
Nothing happens at bind.

Left at reset on purpose: scaling (beyond bypass), colour management, the
17x17x17 LUT, HDR/cdc tone mapping, smart backlight, dynamic gamma and
de-banding, AFBC/TFBD compressed sources, write-back, offline composition,
online rotation, 10-bit and YUV paths, the other six channels, the other three
layers of this channel, and the second display output. Of 29 233 non-zero words
on a live link, 26 315 (90 %) sit on 31 pages that are essentially solid
coefficient and LUT data. Eleven registers carry the framebuffer -- six on the
channel, five on the blender -- and ten of the eleven are computed from the mode.

The manual does not document this block
---------------------------------------

A733 UM V0.92 chapter 14.3 "Display Engine" is two pages of prose with no
register list, offset table or field table. Searched over the whole UM extract:
GLB_CTL, BLD_, OVL_, blender and RCQ return no hits, and the one "mixer" hit is
in 14.5 and describes G2D. TCON_TV (15.2.10) and eDP (15.4.7) have register
chapters; the DE does not.

So no DE register in this patch carries a manual reference. The evidence floor
here is a live capture plus mainline's model of this DE generation, and every
value says which of three things it is:

  COMPUTED  derived from the mode or the framebuffer; the live link agrees
  CARRIED   copied off a working link and not understood
  INFERRED  the name and meaning come from code (mainline's DE33 model, or the
            out-of-tree A523/T527 v35x series) and the live A733 capture then
            confirms the value at that offset

| register                  | value      | tag      | evidence                 |
|---------------------------|------------|----------|--------------------------|
| de_top VERSION (read)     | 0x03520100 | -        | silicon, the 8 GiB board, 08-18 and 08-19 |
| de_top DE2TCON_MUX        | 0x0000fff5 | CARRIED  | silicon; nibble 5 has no known meaning |
| mixer0 GLOBAL_CTL         | BIT(0)     | INFERRED | mainline RT_EN; silicon  |
| mixer0 GLOBAL_SIZE        | 0x04af077f | COMPUTED | ((h-1)<<16)|(w-1)        |
| mixer0 GLOBAL_CLK         | 1          | INFERRED | mainline DE33-only reg   |
| mixer0 RCQ_HEAD_LEN/ADDR  | 0          | COMPUTED | this driver is the only writer |
| vi0 LAY0_ATTCTL           | 0xff008403 | COMPUTED | every field from the format |
| vi0 LAY0_MBSIZE           | 0x04af077f | COMPUTED | the mode                 |
| vi0 LAY0_MBCOOR           | 0          | COMPUTED | origin                   |
| vi0 LAY0_PITCH0           | 0x00001e00 | COMPUTED | 1920 x 4 = 7680          |
| vi0 LAY0_PITCH1/2, LADDR1/2| 0         | COMPUTED | packed RGB has one plane |
| vi0 LAY0_TOP_LADDR0       | allocation | COMPUTED | the framebuffer          |
| vi0 OVL_SIZE              | 0x04af077f | COMPUTED | the mode                 |
| bld PIPE_CTL              | 0x00000101 | COMPUTED | EN(0) | FC_EN(0)         |
| bld FCOLOR0               | 0xff000000 | COMPUTED | AARRGGBB opaque black    |
| bld INSIZE0 / COORD0      | mode / 0   | COMPUTED | full screen at origin    |
| bld ROUTE                 | 0          | COMPUTED | pipe 0 <- channel 0      |
| bld PREMULTIPLY           | 0          | COMPUTED | XRGB has no alpha        |
| bld BKCOLOR               | 0xff000000 | COMPUTED | mainline's init value    |
| bld OUTSIZE               | 0x04af077f | COMPUTED | the mode                 |
| bld BLEND_MODE0           | 0x03010301 | CARRIED  | mainline's own comment calls it "some still unknown magic numbers" |
| vi0 CCSC_CTL x8           | 1          | CARRIED  | silicon                  |
| vi0 VSU_CTL, OUT_SIZE     | 0          | CARRIED  | silicon                  |
| vi0 TFBD                  | not written| -        | read and reported        |

The four blender instances at a 0x400 stride are all written identically and
that is CARRIED: the UM lists two display outputs, so "one per lit output" is
not what four register files are, and the live link holds all four identical
on a board with exactly one pipe up.

DE2TCON_MUX's disp0 nibble is 5. This SoC has four TCONs (LCD0, LCD1, TV0, TV1),
so a plain index would make TCON_TV1 = 3. Two readings survive: a global index
in a numbering space wider than this part populates, or an enable bit plus an
index ([3:1] = 2 with bit 0 set). This patch writes the word a working link
carries; the meaning is open. Check: writing 0x0000fff3 or 0x0000ffff on a live
link and watching the screen settles the encoding.

The three de_top enables open the datapath apertures
----------------------------------------------------

    0x05008000 = 0x00010001
    0x05008004 = 0x00010001
    0x05008008 = 0x00000011

Without them the channel window at 0x0510xxxx and the blender at 0x0528xxxx do
not accept a write at all: they read back zero exactly like unmapped space,
while de_top at 0x05008xxx answers normally. Shown causally by raw devmem with
no driver involved:

    before:  devmem 0x05101000 32 0xFF008403 ; devmem 0x05101000 -> 0x00000000
             (0x05200000, genuinely unmapped, also reads 0x00000000)
    after the three writes:
             devmem 0x05101000 32 0xFF008403 ; devmem 0x05101000 -> 0xFF008403
             devmem 0x05281000 32 0x00000101 ; devmem 0x05281000 -> 0x00000101

The vendor capture shows the three words non-zero on a working link, which is
the evidence that something writes them; a word a live link holds and a cold
block does not is a write, and "not understood" is a reason to carry it and say
so. All three are written and tagged CARRIED, with the bit shape (0x00010001 is
this SoC's de-assert-reset + gate pattern) as the only INFERRED part.

Ordering rule: re-apply after every reset_control_deassert(), before any
datapath write. Two of the three are the DE's inner reset+gate registers, and
RST_BUS_DE0 (DE0_BGR bit 16, this node's own reset) clears them along with
everything else inside the block. Measured: after an `enable 0` / `enable 1`
cycle they read 0x00000000 again, because sun60i_de_clocks_off() asserts exactly
that reset. So sun60i_de_top_open() must not be moved to probe or anywhere else
that runs once. Supplied by hand after `enable`, they let `layer` program the
channel while the blender stayed at zero, because enable had already written it
into a closed window.

sun60i_de_verify() reads back one register per datapath window after the commit
and refuses rather than announcing success. Its two outcomes are different
faults: reading zero means the window is not open (it prints 0x05008000 /
0x05008004 / 0x05008008 with their expected values); reading something else
means the window is open but did not take the write, which is the shadow the
RCQ exists for. `state` lists the three enable words first, with their wanted
values.

On the board, 2026-08-19
------------------------

Run on the 8 GiB board in one kernel with
`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` and
`arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge`, with the three
de_top enables supplied by hand. The observer first: the TCON's own VDBIST
colour bars confirmed on the monitor by eye. Then the DE made the source, and
the screen showed this driver's framebuffer. Then the test that separates "a
picture" from "our picture":

    echo 0x0000FF00 > fill    ->  the screen turned green
                                  with LAY0_TOP_LADDR0 unchanged at 0xFE400000
                                  and no register touched at all

Only DRAM content changed: the DE is fetching this driver's buffer, at this
driver's address, in this driver's format. Final state: both sticky TCON latches
clean, verdict SCANNING, swept to 1233 of VTOTAL 1235 over 4 wraps, underflow 0
and lostwarn 0 before and during. This settled:

  - the direct-MMIO commit path works on a cold DE, which is the limit the
    warm-vendor measurement could not reach. The RCQ stays unnecessary, and the
    commit indirection stays for the narrower reason that one register is not
    every register.
  - the IOMMU risk did not materialise: LAY0_TOP_LADDR0 = 0xFE400000 is a plain
    physical address from dma_alloc_coherent() and the DE fetched it. On this
    kernel the IOMMU is not translating for this master. The vendor's
    0xFC000000 is still an IOVA and not comparable.
  - the clock posture: de0 came up at 480000000 Hz from pll-peri0-480m (the
    pinning of `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge`),
    bus-de0 at 200000000 Hz; the floor check passed and the DE supplied
    1920x1200 at 154 MHz with zero underflows.
  - the mode, the format and the blender constants are right as written:
    ATTCTL 0xFF008403, MBSIZE/OVL_SIZE/INSIZE0/OUTSIZE 0x04AF077F, PITCH
    0x00001E00, PIPE_CTL 0x101, FCOLOR0 0xFF000000, BLEND_MODE0 0x03010301,
    DE2TCON_MUX 0x0000FFF5, VERSION 0x03520100, RCQ_HEAD_LEN 0x00000000.

A later run with `enable` alone, nothing typed after it, programmed the whole
datapath: the three de_top enables read 0x00010001 / 0x00010001 / 0x00000011,
the layer ATTCTL 0xff008403, MBSIZE and OVL_SIZE 0x04af077f, PITCH 0x00001e00,
LADDR0 0xfe400000, and the blender PIPE_CTL 0x00000101, FCOLOR0 0xff000000,
INSIZE0 and OUTSIZE 0x04af077f. The screen was black: TCON source handed over
(TV_SRC_CTL = 0x00000000), TCON scanning to 1234 of VTOTAL 1235, underflow and
lostwarn clear, framebuffer verified red by reading DRAM back (0xFE400000 =
0x00FF0000), monitor receiving signal. `bld0 ROUTE` written by hand to
0x00543210, `layer` toggled and the module clock moved DEPLL3X 696 MHz ->
PERI0_480M 480 MHz: no change.

The CCSC matrix
---------------

A diff against the vendor's live link -- 74 registers read at the addresses its
capture holds non-zero in the pixel-path blocks -- shows 24 words left at zero
here: three per CCSC instance, at +0x10, +0x24 and +0x38, all reading
0x00020000, in all eight instances. Under a 3-row x 4-word row-major layout
(three coefficients and one constant per row, rows on a 0x10 stride from +0x10)
these are the diagonal c00, c11, c22:

    c00 = 0x10 + 0*0x10 + 0*4 = +0x10   non-zero in the capture
    c11 = 0x10 + 1*0x10 + 1*4 = +0x24   non-zero in the capture
    c22 = 0x10 + 2*0x10 + 2*4 = +0x38   non-zero in the capture

and the nine off-diagonal words that layout predicts are all zero in the same
capture. That is a unity matrix with 0x00020000 as its "1". The page's word
count agrees: 32 non-zero words on 0x100000 = 8 x (1 control + 3 diagonal).

Why this explains the black screen: with CCSC_CTL = 1 and the matrix at its
cold reset value of zero, an enabled colour-space converter outputs zero for
every pixel. Data still flows, so the TCON never underflows and never sets
lostwarn; the layer, blender, sizes and scanout address read back correct; the
framebuffer contains red; and no clock change can help.

The vendor also writes 24 coefficient words, so it configures this block; 1 is
the vendor's value and not necessarily the reset value, and the A523/T527
series writes ctl = 0 for a linear-RGB layer. Both are reachable from debugfs
`ccsc`: 1 (default) writes the vendor's configuration, enabled with the unity
matrix; 0 disables the block as the prior art does. The three coefficients are
in `state`. This is a hypothesis; it has not been on a board. The rest of the
diff was analysed: 0x05008104 is mainline's read-only GLOBAL_STATUS (a symptom,
not writable), 0x05008098 looks like a counter, and the remaining four are
ranked into a bisect order. All 30 deltas applied in one batch wedged the board
and lost the attribution, so this patch changes exactly one thing.

The delivery question: RCQ or plain stores
------------------------------------------

The v35x DE has a register command queue: a DMA engine that walks 16-byte
descriptors in DRAM and copies register blocks into the DE on each frame-start
pulse from the bound TCON. The vendor's live link has one armed -- head array at
DRAM 0xffff3fc0, RCQ_HEAD_LEN 0x760 = 118 x 16 exactly. The A523/T527 series
for this DE version states that the datapath latches only through that queue and
that plain CPU stores land in a shadow which never latches. If that held here,
every datapath register above would be the right content delivered the wrong
way.

Measured on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-19, on a fresh vendor boot with the
vendor's driver resident and its DE live, in this order:

  0. The observer first: the monitor confirmed by eye to be showing the console
     shell, before any write.
  1. devmem 0x05101018 32 0xFC400000 (+4 MiB) -- the register holds, and the
     screen goes black, monitor still on and not in standby.
  2. devmem 0x05101018 32 0xFC000000 -- the shell comes back.

A plain MMIO write to the VI layer's scanout address reaches the datapath. Step
0 matters: a black screen from a monitor that is off is indistinguishable from
one the write caused, so the observer is established first; a first run of this
sequence without that was discarded and redone. The acceptance sequence below
carries the same order: step 6 (`fill`) writes a colour into the framebuffer
and touches no register, so it proves the observer and the fetch path in one
command, and runs before step 7 moves a register.

Two limits survive, and they are why the commit path stays an indirection:

  1. it proves the VI layer's scanout address latches, not that every DE
     register does. If some other block's write appears not to take effect, the
     RCQ is back on the table for that block.
  2. the write was made while the vendor driver held the device (queue armed,
     datapath loaded). A cold-programmed DE, whose queue was never armed, is
     what this patch produces; step 7 of the acceptance run tests it.

struct sun60i_de_commit is three ops -- begin, stage, arm -- and direct MMIO is
the real implementation. de_top and the mixer top latch directly under every
reading of this hardware; the blender and the channel are what the queue exists
for. So the control plane is plain writel() and only the datapath goes through
the ops. An RCQ backend would stage (offset, value) pairs into a coherent block,
build a head array, and arm the queue in arm() without moving a register value.
For scale, the prior art's first sun55i_de.c is ~39 KB and is followed by
roughly 200 KB of patches whose titles are almost entirely RCQ concerns
(page-flip tearing with the queue armed, full reload after modeset, pool out of
low CMA, holding old framebuffers until the arm, block retry, races with the
arm, channel quiesce before stopping). This patch does not need that machinery
because MMIO is measured to reach the datapath.

The driver also disarms the queue before touching the datapath, length to zero
first, so no walk can start from a valid length and a half-written pointer. On a
cold DE those are four no-op stores; they cover a queue left armed by a
bootloader or a previous kernel, which would reload the datapath from stale
DRAM once per frame.

The vendor path runs through an IOMMU; this one does not
--------------------------------------------------------

Measured on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-19 with the DE live: 5000000.de sits in
iommu_group 0 alongside the VE and the deinterlacer; sunxi-iommu-v2 at
3900000.iommu comes up with "Default domain type: Translated" and a strict TLB
invalidation policy; and the vendor DT gives de@5000000 `iommus = <&iommu 8 1>`
-- master ID 8. It is a translation and not an identity map: 0x05101018 reads
0xFC000000 with a picture on the screen, and a physical read at 0xFC000000
returns 0x005E0001 / 0xFE40FB00 / 0x005E0001 at +0/+4/+0x1000 -- structured
data, not pixels. System RAM is 0x40000000-0x23FFFFFFF, so the address is in
range; it is not where the framebuffer physically lives.

Consequences:

  1. Scanout addresses in a vendor capture are not comparable to this driver's:
     0xFC000000 there is an IOVA. Every other register in this patch can be
     diffed against that capture.
  2. This tree has no IOMMU support: no patch mentions one, no DT node exists,
     no `iommus` property is anywhere in it. The DE's address therefore reaches
     the bus untranslated, which is correct if the IOMMU is in bypass or off,
     and that is not verified in general (it held on the 2026-08-19 run). If the
     IOMMU came up translating with no mapping for master 8, the DE would fetch
     from nowhere, with every register reading back correctly. Mainline 6.18.36
     ships drivers/iommu/sun50i-iommu.c; whether it matches this SoC's
     sunxi-iommu-v2 is unverified.
  3. The driver uses the DMA API, so it is written for either case:
     dma_alloc_coherent() returns a dma_addr_t that is a physical address today
     and becomes an IOVA when an IOMMU node and an `iommus` property exist, with
     no change in this file. It asks device_iommu_mapped() and says which it
     got, at enable and in `state`:

       scanout buffer at 0x........ -- a physical address, no IOMMU is attached
       scanout buffer at 0x........ -- an IOVA, this device is behind an IOMMU

The patch does not ask the device tree for an `iommus` property: a phandle to an
IOMMU node with no driver bound leaves the device deferring forever. It arrives
together with a verified IOMMU driver for this SoC.

Mainline and prior art
----------------------

Mainline 6.18.36 already models this display-engine generation:
SUN8I_MIXER_DE33 in drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun8i_mixer.h:157-161, compatible
"allwinner,sun50i-h616-de33-mixer-0" at sun8i_mixer.c:859, with the channel
formula map[channel] * 0x20000 + 0x1000. This silicon decodes under it register
for register, including mainline's unexplained 0x03010301. What this patch takes
from it is the decoding key -- field layouts, bit positions, the size macro, the
format number -- re-derived against the capture. No code is copied and no
mainline file is touched.

The intended direction is for the DE to join sun4i-drm as a sunxi_engine
backend. This patch does not do that yet, for testability:

  - sun4i_drv.c is a component master driven by a display-engine node with
    allwinner,pipelines, and sun4i_tcon_bind() returns -EPROBE_DEFER without a
    matching mixer and display-engine (sun4i_tcon.c:1121-1125). The smallest
    working unit there is the whole KMS stack, so a failure would not say
    whether the eleven registers or the plumbing were wrong.
  - sun4i_crtc_init() takes a struct sun4i_tcon *, so reusing that CRTC means
    folding the TCON patch into sun4i_tcon.c or refactoring shared mainline code
    that seven other SoCs use.
  - sun8i_mixer_cfg.map[] is sized 6 and a full A733 needs 7.
  - the device tree for any of that belongs to a separate patch.

So the register set is proven first, in isolation, with a driver shaped like the
one that proved the TCON; the move into sun4i-drm is then a change of frame
around a known-good register set. Revisit once this is HW-proven.

The three blocks whose live state matters, and what the driver does
-------------------------------------------------------------------

All three words are read on a cold DE before anything is written and reported
by debugfs `state`, which is also the first DE reset-state dump this tree has.

1. The per-channel scaler (VSU, slot + 0x4000). The prior art reports the v35x
   channel datapath as always overlay -> VSU -> blender with no bypass, and that
   after power-gating and re-ungating the DE the filter-coefficient SRAM powers
   up holding random data; the stated reason the vendor never hits this is that
   Android never power-gates the DE. This capture cannot settle it, because the
   vendor stack never power-gated it either -- and this driver does, on every
   enable. It writes VSU_CTL = 0, which is what a working link carries, and does
   not load a unity coefficient table: the VSU register map is prior art only,
   unconfirmed at this offset on this part. Check: a picture that is present
   but visibly corrupt (the series describes a repeated column) would mean
   coefficients are owed.

2. The channel CSC (slot + 0x800) reads 0x00000001 -- enabled -- in eight
   instances at a 0x100 stride; see the CCSC section above. The A523 series
   writes 0 for a linear-RGB layer; silicon on this part carries 1 while
   producing correct colour. Check: wrong colour (channels swapped, washed
   out); `state`'s cold CCSC line says which way to go.

3. The tiled-framebuffer decoder (TFBD, slot + 0x5400) reads 0x00002103 in four
   instances at a 0x100 stride, including slot 6, a channel that was fetching
   nothing, so it looks like an idle value. It is read and reported, not
   written. The series warns that a stale TFBD "mangles the fetch" and clears
   it, but its register map is unconfirmed here and this framebuffer is linear.
   Check: a fetch that produces tiled or garbage output while every register
   reads back correctly.

PREMULTIPLY = 0 and BKCOLOR = opaque black are written although the live link
leaves them at zero. PREMULTIPLY = 0 is the value either way and is written so a
bootloader cannot leave it set for a format with no alpha. BKCOLOR is what
mainline initialises; it is invisible behind a full-screen opaque layer and is
the first thing that shows when a plane is smaller than the screen (the
out-of-tree series added a patch titled "black blender background" for that
reason).

BLD_OUTCTL, at +0xfc, is mainline's interlace bit, but the A523 block map puts
+0xa0..+0xff in the colour-key region, so the two candidate names conflict and
the capture cannot choose: it reads zero, which is correct under both. This mode
is progressive, so the register is not written.

Differences from the prior art
------------------------------

Neither body of code is ported; both are decoding keys. Where the A523/T527 v35x
series and this driver differ:

  - the series reuses mainline's H616 channel map {0, 6, 7, 8}; this driver uses
    slots {0,1,2,6,7,8,9}, measured on this silicon: seven scaler pages on an
    exact 0x20000 stride, against the UM's seven blending channels (three VI,
    four UI).
  - the series puts the layer address at +0x10, as for a UI channel; this driver
    uses +0x18, because slot 0 is a VI channel with three plane pitches and
    three plane addresses. Mixing the two points the scanout at a fill-colour
    register in the other layout; both maps are internally consistent.
  - the series writes the channel CSC control to 0 for a linear-RGB layer; this
    driver writes 1, which silicon carries while producing correct colour, and
    reports the cold value.
  - the series clears TFBD unconditionally on a register map written for another
    SoC; this driver reads and reports it.
  - the series' RCQ failure-mode patches are not needed here because MMIO is
    measured to reach the datapath. The seam that would add them for one block
    is three function pointers.

The comparison with the vendor DRM:

  - DRM_SUNXI_PQ_PROC is a custom tuning ioctl; this driver has none (tuning
    would be a property or a debugfs file).
  - cdc (HDR tone mapping, 55 617 lines), smbl (smart backlight, 25 083) and an
    unbuilt panic screen with a bitmap font (17 560) add up to 98 260 lines;
    none is needed here.
  - 757 KB of PQ tuning tables sit in .data; this driver has no tables.
  - the vendor port needs .atomic_precheck, a drm_plane_helper_funcs hook that
    does not exist upstream; the same checks fit in atomic_check.
  - the port notes ask for DRM_OBJECT_MAX_PROPERTY 24 -> 64; it is already 64 in
    6.18.36 (drm_mode_object.h:63).

Where the comparison does not hold: the A523 series has page flip, vblank,
plane state, scaler coefficients, format conversion and a working KMS device.
This patch has none of those; it is one static layer brought up from debugfs.
Its contribution is the evidence per value and the measurement that takes the
RCQ off the critical path, not coverage.

The device tree this consumes
-----------------------------

The DE clocks and the DE node come from separate patches in this series
(`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` and
`arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge`), so this patch
contains no DTS and no CCU. It consumes what they describe:

    mixer0: mixer@5100000 {
            compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-de-mixer-0";
            reg = <0x0 0x05100000 0x0 0x00180000>,
                  <0x0 0x05008100 0x0 0x00000040>,
                  <0x0 0x05280000 0x0 0x00020000>,
                  <0x0 0x05008000 0x0 0x00000100>;
            reg-names = "layers", "top", "display", "de-top";
            interrupts = <GIC_SPI 50 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
            clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_DE0>, <&ccu CLK_DE0>;
            clock-names = "bus", "mod";
            resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_DE0>;
            assigned-clocks = <&ccu CLK_DE0>;
            assigned-clock-parents = <&ccu CLK_PLL_PERI0_480M>;
            ports { ... port@1 -> tcon_tv1 ... };
    };

The node uses mainline's three window names plus "de-top" for the DE-global
page, which mainline's model has no window for and this SoC needs, because
DE2TCON_MUX lives there; a node of its own for the same block would be a worse
device tree.

The compatible is claimed deliberately: this driver is a stand-in for a
sun8i_mixer entry. When that entry lands, one patch moves the string from this
driver's of_match_table into sun8i_mixer_of_table and the device tree does not
change.

The window split is checked at probe, with the address in the message. Every
offset in the driver counts from the DE aperture base (0x05000000), which is
what the capture, the register map and the RCQ's `reg_offset` word use;
sun60i_de_map() is the single place the four windows exist. Probe requires each
named window to land on the expected offset and to be at least as long as the
driver reaches, every minimum computed from the code. A window at the wrong
address on this SoC does not fault or fail to map; it reads zeros or other
registers, so the check turns a DT mistake into a line in dmesg.

It looks up the four reg windows by name, "bus" and "mod", and one reset
unnamed (index 0). Three properties are deliberately not read: `interrupts`
(INTID 82 = GIC_SPI 50 per the A733 user manual; nothing here services one, the
same choice as TV_GINT0 in the TCON patch), `ports` (the mixer binding's
required output edge, from which sun8i_mixer_of_get_id() derives the mixer
index), and `assigned-clock-parents`, which the clock core applies before probe.

Clock constraints
-----------------

  1. This driver does not call clk_set_rate() on the module clock. DE0_CLK's
     source mux is DEPLL3X / DEPLL4X / PERI0_480M / PERI0_400M / PERI0_300M /
     VIDEO0PLL4X / VIDEO2PLL4X, and UM 4.1.4.3 advises against changing DEPLL3X,
     because it also feeds NSI, the SoC interconnect, at 700 MHz. A set_rate that
     propagated to that parent would move the fabric clock under every other
     block. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` reaches the
     same conclusion from the CCU side (CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT on de0, no
     CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT in the chain). The reset value closes on the UM's own
     number: PLL_DE_CTRL_REG reset 0x40125600 gives N=87, M1=1, P1=3 over the
     24 MHz PLL_REF, so DEPLL3X = 24 x 87 / 3 = 696 MHz, "696MHz by default".
     VIDEO1PLL4X is absent from that mux, and it is the net
     `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` retunes to 308 MHz for
     the 154 MHz pixel clock, so the DE and the eDP pixel path do not contend for
     the same PLL.
  2. The driver reads the rate back, refuses below the rate this mode fetches at
     (154 MHz), and logs what it got.

Which parent the DE runs from, measured on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-19 with
the vendor's display engine live (DE version 0x03520100, VI scanout
0x05101018 = 0xFC000000, picture on the monitor):

    PLL_DE_CTRL   0x020022E0 = 0xFD124A00   bit31 set -- PLL_DE is on
    DE0_CLK       0x02002A00 = 0x80000000   gate on, mux [26:24] = 000 = DEPLL3X,
                                            FACTOR_M [4:0] = 0 -> M = 1
    DEPLL gates   0x02003928 = 0x00030000   bits 17:16, the DE PLL tap gates

So the vendor's working DE runs from DEPLL3X, the reset default of that mux.
Decoding 0x??124A00: N field 0x4A -> N = 75, M1 = 1, P1 = 3, so the VCO is
24 x 75 = 1800 MHz and DEPLL3X is 600 MHz, which is also the mod_rate mainline's
DE33 config carries for the H616. The field map is cross-checked against the
UM: the reset value 0x40125600 decodes as 696 MHz, matching "696MHz by default".

A reading taken on 2026-07-18 on a board whose DE was down shows PLL_DE off; a
reading of an idle block says nothing about a running one, and the live reading
above is the one that counts.

For this driver that changes nothing in the register set, and one thing in its
posture: the DE node pins PERI0_480M, the vendor uses DEPLL3X at 600 MHz, and
both clear this mode's 154 MHz floor. The driver is written for neither: it
never sets a rate, reads back what it was given, refuses below the floor, and
prints the rate at enable so a board session records which parent was in effect.

The failure path names addresses, because four faults read alike. A gated block
on this SoC answers 0x00000000 without faulting, so a zero cannot tell a wrong
base from a reset still asserted from a shut gate from a source clock that is
not running, and clk_get_rate() cannot settle it either: a sunxi PLL's recalc
computes from its register fields whether or not PLL_EN is set. So the rate is
printed as evidence, not as a verdict, and sun60i_de_check_alive() prints all
four addresses with the values a live vendor DE carries:

    DE0_BGR      0x02002A04   expect 0x00010001
    DE0_CLK      0x02002A00   bit31 gate, [26:24] mux, [4:0] M   (live 0x80000000)
    PLL_DE_CTRL  0x020022E0   bit31 PLL_EN                       (live 0xFD124A00)
    DE tap gates 0x02003928   bits 17:16                         (live 0x00030000)

The last two matter when the mux selects a DE PLL tap, which the vendor's own
live DE does. A dead TCON_TV1 raster on 2026-08-19 was diagnosed in ten minutes
because the driver announced a missing reg window and clock with their
addresses; every window, clock and reset this driver expects is named the same
way.

Nothing else is owed to the CCU: DE_SYS_BGR (0x0A74), the AHB matrix gate (0x05C0
bit 5), the MBUS matrix gate (0x05E0 bit 11, the path both the scanout fetch and
any RCQ descriptor fetch use) and CM_DESYS_CFG (0x1B04) are in their working
state at power-on and the vendor leaves all four alone.

Acceptance sequence
-------------------

Steps 1-7 passed on 2026-08-19 on the board with the three de_top enables
supplied by hand. The re-run has to show the driver supplies them itself: the
same sequence with nothing typed between `enable` and the picture. Step 4 must
show the three enable words non-zero and every datapath line non-zero without
intervention, and step 3 must not print the "window is NOT OPEN" error. Step 5
still owes its own observable: the 08-19 run filled flat colours, which prove
fetch but cannot show a geometry error. The gradient inside a one-pixel white
border, which `enable` paints before anything is typed, discriminates, and has
not been looked at yet.

Needs the DE node above, a sink on J4, the port in a DP pin assignment, and the
link trained. Run in this order; each step is a gate on the next.

1. Baseline -- the TCON scans its own pattern.

     echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5731000.lcd-controller/enable
     cat     /sys/kernel/debug/5731000.lcd-controller/scanout

   Pass: verdict SCANNING, maximum 1235, at least one wrap, underflow and
   lostwarn 0, and colour bars on the screen. Fails if: no bars. Stop; that is
   the TCON, transmitter or link training and nothing below means anything.

2. The driver bound at all:

     ls -d /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer

   Fails if: absent, with dmesg carrying `reg "<name>" is at 0x........,
   expected 0x........` or `reg "<name>" is N bytes, this driver reaches M`.
   That is the device tree's window decomposition disagreeing with this
   driver's, named at probe.

3. The DE comes up cold.

     echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/enable
     dmesg | tail -5

   Pass: "module clock <N> Hz (floor 154000000)" and "scanning 1920x1200
   XRGB8888 from 0x........, pitch 7680, commit by direct MMIO".
   Fails (A): "DE version reads 0x00000000, expected 0x03520100 -- the block is
     not answering", followed by four lines naming the rate and the four
     addresses. Read what the driver printed, in this order:

       0x02002A04 DE0_BGR      expect 0x00010001
       0x02002A00 DE0_CLK      expect bit31 set   (a live vendor DE: 0x80000000)
       0x020022E0 PLL_DE_CTRL  bit31 PLL_EN       (live 0xFD124A00) -- only if
       0x02003928 DE tap gates bits 17:16         (live 0x00030000)    the mux
                                                  selects a DE PLL tap
       cat /sys/kernel/debug/clk/de0/clk_parent

     The eDP block measured the reset/gate half of this symptom for the same
     cause. The base is the last thing to doubt.
   Fails (B): "module clock is <N> Hz, below the 154000000 Hz this mode fetches
     at" -- the DT's parent for CLK_DE0.
   Fails (C): "no 9216000-byte coherent framebuffer" -- the CMA area is too
     small or too fragmented.

4. The register state. cat .../5100000.mixer/state

   Expected, exactly:
     de_top VERSION        0x03520100     vi0 OVL_SIZE       0x04af077f
     de_top DE2TCON_MUX    0x0000fff5     bld0 PIPE_CTL      0x00000101
     mixer0 GLOBAL_CTL     0x00000001     bld0 FCOLOR0       0xff000000
     mixer0 GLOBAL_SIZE    0x04af077f     bld0 INSIZE0       0x04af077f
     mixer0 GLOBAL_CLK     0x00000001     bld0 ROUTE         0x00000000
     mixer0 RCQ_HEAD_LEN   0x00000000     bld0 BKCOLOR       0xff000000
     vi0 LAY0_ATTCTL       0xff008403     bld0 OUTSIZE       0x04af077f
     vi0 LAY0_MBSIZE       0x04af077f     bld0 BLEND_MODE0   0x03010301
     vi0 LAY0_MBCOOR       0x00000000
     vi0 LAY0_PITCH0       0x00001e00
     vi0 LAY0_TOP_LADDR0   = the address the "scanning" line printed

   These read-backs are not the proof; step 5 is. Fails if: a datapath register
   reads back something other than what was written while the control plane
   reads back correctly. That is the shadow the 2026-08-19 measurement says does
   not exist on this path, and since that measurement was taken on a warm DE,
   this outcome puts an RCQ backend behind the commit ops. Record which
   registers; the answer may be per block. Also record the "read on a COLD DE"
   line: the version, CCSC, VSU and TFBD reset values, which decide the traps
   above.

5. The proof, by eye.

     echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5731000.lcd-controller/source

   A person looking at the screen must see the colour bars replaced by a smooth
   gradient -- dark at the top-left, red increasing to the right, green
   increasing downward, a constant low blue -- inside a one-pixel white border on
   all four edges. Nothing VDBIST can produce looks like that.
   Fails, black screen: the DE is not reaching the TCON. Re-read `scanout`: a
     line counter still sweeping to 1235 means the TCON is fine and the DE is
     supplying nothing (DE2TCON_MUX or the blender route); a stopped counter
     means the TCON stopped when its source changed.
   Fails, bars persist: TV_SRC_CTL bit 0 did not move. Read it back.
   Fails, the white border is a diagonal or the picture repeats horizontally:
     the pitch. LAY0_PITCH0 must be 0x1e00.
   Fails, the border is off the edge or the image is doubled vertically:
     MBSIZE / OVL_SIZE / the blender sizes against the TCON's active area.
   Fails, a picture collapsed to a repeated column: VSU_CTL = 0 does not bypass
     on a cold DE, and unity coefficients are owed.
   Fails, the gradient runs in the wrong channel (blue varying, or a coloured
     border): the format field or the channel CSC; the cold CCSC value from step
     4 says which.
   Fails, tiled blocks or noise: TFBD is stale after all.
   Fails, black screen with every register reading back correct: check whether
     the address the DE was given is one it can reach. Read the driver's line
     from step 3 ("a physical address, no IOMMU is attached" vs "an IOVA") and
     check 0x03900000 / 0x03910000 for an IOMMU that came up translating with no
     mapping for master 8. That failure looks like a wrong register sequence
     from the register side alone.
   Fails, `scanout` reports underflow during the window: the DE is not
     delivering pixels fast enough -- the module clock or the MBUS path.

6. Causality, and it proves the observer -- run this before step 7.

     echo 0xff0000ff > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/fill   # blue
     echo 0xff00ff00 > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/fill   # green

   Pass: the screen follows each write, and no register is written -- this only
   touches the framebuffer. It retires two confounds: the DE is fetching this
   memory, and a human is watching a monitor that is switched on.
   Fails if: the screen does not change. Then step 5 proved nothing about the
   fetch path.

7. The cold DE -- does a CPU store reach its datapath too? The vendor-side
   measurement covered a warm DE with its queue armed. Only valid if step 6
   passed.

     echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/layer    # one datapath register
     cat   /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/state       # what it reads back
     echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/layer    # and back again

   Result A -- the picture disappears and returns: MMIO latches the datapath of
     a cold DE too; the commit backend is complete and the RCQ is not needed.
   Result B -- the picture does not change while `state` shows `vi0 LAY0_ATTCTL`
     reading back what was written (0x00000000 then 0xff008403): that is the
     shadow the A523/T527 series describes and the RCQ is mandatory. The
     register values are the right content; the work is one backend behind
     begin/stage/arm plus the flip/retire/quiesce discipline estimated above.
   Either result is a finding, and B costs no register value.

8. If the screen is still black -- one write, two readings.

     cat   /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/state    # the three CCSC lines
     echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/ccsc  # disable the block instead

   Pass (either way): the picture appears. With ccsc = 1 and the coefficients
   reading 0x00020000, the vendor's configuration is right; if it takes
   ccsc = 0, the A523/T527 reading is right for this SoC and the default flips
   in a one-line follow-up. Fails if: black under both; then the CCSC is not on
   the path, and the bisect moves to the capture-diff entry at 0x05008030, one
   group at a time (thirty deltas in one batch lost the attribution on
   2026-08-19 and wedged the board).

9. The negative control.

     echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/enable   # picture goes
     echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/enable   # picture back

   Pass: the screen loses the picture and gets it back. A second enable also
   exercises the disarm and the stop-before-programming path, which a first
   enable off a fresh reset cannot.

Hardware status
---------------

Proven (the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-19): the register set, the mode, the format,
the direct-MMIO commit path on a cold DE, the fetch of this driver's own buffer
at its own physical address, zero data-supply errors, and sun60i_de_top_open()
plus sun60i_de_verify() with one `enable` and nothing typed after it. Not
proven: the CCSC unity matrix in this revision, which is a derivation from the
capture, and the gradient/border geometry test. Applies at --fuzz=0 on the full
series; drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/ builds W=1 warning-free with
CONFIG_DRM_SUN60I_DE=m cross-compiled for arm64; checkpatch --strict reports 0
errors, 0 warnings, 0 checks.

Correction on the high-address bytes: VI +0x0d0..+0x0e4 (TOP_HADDR0-2,
BOT_HADDR0-2) carry one byte per layer, and a 1 in layer 1's byte moved that
layer's fetch 4 GiB up (DP sink CRC with a control, 8 GB board). An earlier
search that found no such register read 0x05101080, which is layer 2's plane-2
address. So probe()'s 32-bit DMA mask is this driver's choice -- it writes no
high-address byte -- not a hardware limit. The refusal string "... the layer
address register is 32 bits" still gives the old reason.

Assisted-by: Claude

